# Closing Issues from a PR

GitHub closes an Issue when a closing verb (`close(s/d)`, `fix(es/ed)`, `resolve(s/d)`) stands directly before its number, even in a negated sentence.

- A PR that finishes an Issue puts `Closes #N` alone on a line under `## Related`.
- A PR that does not finish it never puts a closing verb before `#N`. Write `Part of #N` or `Refs #N`.
- The line lives in the PR body only, never in a commit body: the squash message concatenates commit bodies.
- After a PR is created or its body edited, run `gh pr view <n> --json closingIssuesReferences` and compare the list with the intent.

```
# BAD: this closes #120
This does not close #120.

# GOOD
Part of #120. The acceptance text on #120 is still unmet.
```

When the PR body itself says a condition of the Issue is not met, the PR does not close the Issue as it stands. Before the PR is opened, either the unmet conditions are moved to an Issue and a roadmap row of their own and the first Issue's text is changed to say so, or the PR says `Part of #N`. Which of the two is the owner's to say when the conditions are ones the owner set. #137 was opened with `Closes #132` and two of three conditions unmet; the review caught it, and the two became #138.

Skill: `.agents/skills/github-closing/SKILL.md`.
